WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.120
Back to the prep room.

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:04.720
A pale blue precipitate, a deep blue complex

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:07.600
and a blue-green flame told us one bottle

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:08.880
holds copper ions.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:10.840
But copper what?

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:14.400
Copper chloride, copper sulfate and copper acetate all

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:17.280
dissolve, and all give the same copper results.

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:19.760
The cation's only half the answer.

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:22.320
Now we need the anion, and the list

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:27.200
has eight: chloride, bromide, iodide, hydroxide,

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:30.560
acetate, carbonate, sulfate and phosphate.

00:00:32.567 --> 00:00:35.286
The plan's the same as for cations: fresh

00:00:35.367 --> 00:00:38.086
portions, and reagents that react with only a

00:00:38.167 --> 00:00:39.286
few ions.

00:00:39.607 --> 00:00:41.286
Start with dilute acid.

00:00:41.607 --> 00:00:44.646
Carbonate ions take hydrogen ions and break down

00:00:44.767 --> 00:00:47.286
into carbon dioxide and water, so the tube

00:00:47.367 --> 00:00:48.486
fizzes.

00:00:48.607 --> 00:00:50.886
Bubble the gas through limewater, and it turns

00:00:50.967 --> 00:00:53.766
milky as calcium carbonate forms.

00:00:53.887 --> 00:00:57.766
Fizzing that turns limewater milky points to carbonate.

00:00:57.887 --> 00:01:00.486
Not every anion test makes a precipitate.

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:05.700
Acetate takes hydrogen ions too, but it doesn't

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:06.980
make a gas.

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:10.660
It becomes ethanoic acid, the acid in vinegar.

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:13.140
So there's no fizz, but if you warm

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:16.260
the tube gently, you'll notice a vinegar smell.

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:18.580
Waft it towards you, don't sniff straight from

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:19.620
the tube.

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:22.740
To confirm, add neutral iron three chloride to

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:24.340
a fresh portion.

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:27.060
Acetate ions bond around the iron ions and

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:30.100
form a complex with a deep red-brown colour.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.900
The next tests use barium ions and silver

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:35.700
ions.

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:38.820
But carbonate forms solids with both, so it

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:40.700
could fake a positive result.

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:43.300
The fix is to acidify first.

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:47.060
The acid turns any carbonate into carbon dioxide,

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:48.500
so it can't precipitate.

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:51.340
Here's the catch: which acid?

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:54.660
For the sulfate test, hydrochloric acid is fine,

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:57.140
since chloride doesn't precipitate with barium.

00:01:57.620 --> 00:01:59.540
But in the halide test, it would add

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:02.260
chloride, the very ion you're testing for.

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:04.980
So use dilute nitric acid.

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:08.580
Nitrates are all soluble, so nitrate can't fake

00:02:08.607 --> 00:02:09.220
a result.

00:02:11.234 --> 00:02:12.273
Now sulfate.

00:02:12.514 --> 00:02:16.193
Acidify a fresh portion, then add barium chloride

00:02:16.274 --> 00:02:18.033
or barium nitrate solution.

00:02:18.514 --> 00:02:22.193
A white precipitate of barium sulfate means sulfate.

00:02:22.434 --> 00:02:24.513
It stays put in the acid, where barium

00:02:24.674 --> 00:02:26.113
carbonate would have dissolved.

00:02:26.594 --> 00:02:28.433
It's the same white solid you weighed in

00:02:28.514 --> 00:02:32.353
gravimetric analysis, so this reaction both detects sulfate

00:02:32.594 --> 00:02:33.713
and measures it.

00:02:35.734 --> 00:02:38.613
For the halides, acidify a fresh portion with

00:02:38.694 --> 00:02:41.893
nitric acid, then add silver nitrate.

00:02:42.214 --> 00:02:44.453
The halides are the exception in the solubility

00:02:44.614 --> 00:02:47.413
rules when they meet silver, so silver locks

00:02:47.614 --> 00:02:49.373
onto any halide present.

00:02:49.654 --> 00:02:52.053
Each halide gives a silver solid, and the

00:02:52.214 --> 00:02:54.413
colour deepens as you go down the group.

00:02:54.694 --> 00:02:56.613
Silver chloride is white.

00:02:56.774 --> 00:02:58.773
Silver bromide is cream.

00:02:58.854 --> 00:03:01.013
Silver iodide is pale yellow.

00:03:03.034 --> 00:03:05.353
Cream and pale yellow can look alike in

00:03:05.394 --> 00:03:07.913
a test tube, so confirm with ammonia.

00:03:08.474 --> 00:03:11.273
Ammonia molecules bond to silver ions and form

00:03:11.314 --> 00:03:13.913
a complex, which pulls the solid back into

00:03:13.994 --> 00:03:14.553
solution.

00:03:15.034 --> 00:03:17.673
The less soluble the silver halide, the harder

00:03:17.754 --> 00:03:18.513
that is.

00:03:18.794 --> 00:03:21.673
Silver chloride dissolves in dilute ammonia.

00:03:21.914 --> 00:03:24.673
Silver bromide needs concentrated ammonia.

00:03:24.874 --> 00:03:28.153
And silver iodide stays put, even in concentrated

00:03:28.234 --> 00:03:28.873
ammonia.

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:33.860
Phosphate gives a yellow silver solid too, silver

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:34.780
phosphate.

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:36.340
So how do you keep it apart from

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:37.300
iodide?

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:40.420
Test phosphate in a neutral solution, not an

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:41.300
acidified one.

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:44.900
In acid, phosphate ions pick up hydrogen ions

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:47.780
and stop precipitating with silver, which is also

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:50.420
why acidifying keeps phosphate out of the halide

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:51.180
test.

00:03:51.460 --> 00:03:54.100
So add silver nitrate to a neutral portion,

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:56.500
and a yellow precipitate suggests phosphate.

00:03:56.980 --> 00:03:58.900
Then add dilute nitric acid.

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:02.820
Silver phosphate dissolves, but silver iodide doesn't.

00:04:04.834 --> 00:04:06.513
Last, hydroxide.

00:04:06.994 --> 00:04:09.953
A hydroxide solution is basic, so it turns

00:04:10.034 --> 00:04:11.553
red litmus blue.

00:04:11.874 --> 00:04:14.993
But carbonate and phosphate solutions are basic too,

00:04:15.074 --> 00:04:17.073
so rule those out first.

00:04:17.354 --> 00:04:19.793
Then warm a portion with an ammonium salt,

00:04:19.914 --> 00:04:21.713
like ammonium chloride.

00:04:21.794 --> 00:04:24.833
Hydroxide ions take a hydrogen ion from each

00:04:24.914 --> 00:04:28.033
ammonium ion and release ammonia gas.

00:04:28.354 --> 00:04:30.433
Hold damp red litmus paper at the mouth

00:04:30.514 --> 00:04:32.513
of the tube, and it turns blue.

00:04:34.534 --> 00:04:36.853
Here's the full anion flowchart.

00:04:37.174 --> 00:04:40.693
Dilute acid first: a fizz for carbonate, a

00:04:40.774 --> 00:04:42.933
vinegar smell on warming for acetate.

00:04:43.574 --> 00:04:47.333
Then acidified barium for sulfate, and acidified silver

00:04:47.414 --> 00:04:49.893
for the halides, with ammonia to split them.

00:04:50.454 --> 00:04:53.493
Then neutral silver for phosphate, and the ammonium

00:04:53.574 --> 00:04:55.493
test for hydroxide, last.

00:04:56.054 --> 00:04:58.693
Each test uses a fresh portion, so one

00:04:58.774 --> 00:05:00.693
reagent can't spoil the next.

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:05.500
To keep it straight: for barium and halide

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:08.860
tests, acid first, using an acid that adds

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:11.260
none of the ions you're testing for.

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:14.300
Phosphate needs a separate neutral portion.

00:05:14.420 --> 00:05:17.340
Then the silver halides go white, cream, pale

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:20.460
yellow, and ammonia dissolves them in dilute, then

00:05:20.540 --> 00:05:22.380
concentrated, then neither.

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:25.320
Your turn.

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:28.560
A student tests for chloride by acidifying with

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:32.080
hydrochloric acid, then adding silver nitrate.

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:35.440
Explain why the conclusion isn't valid, and describe

00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:36.640
a better test.

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:38.240
Pause here to answer.

00:05:49.467 --> 00:05:52.586
The hydrochloric acid brought its own chloride, so

00:05:52.667 --> 00:05:55.066
white silver chloride would form whether the sample

00:05:55.147 --> 00:05:56.626
had chloride or not.

00:05:56.907 --> 00:05:59.786
Acidify a fresh portion with dilute nitric acid

00:06:00.067 --> 00:06:02.546
instead, then add silver nitrate.

00:06:02.827 --> 00:06:05.546
A white precipitate that dissolves in dilute ammonia

00:06:05.627 --> 00:06:07.306
supports chloride.

00:06:07.547 --> 00:06:09.706
Thinking any acid will do, because it only

00:06:09.787 --> 00:06:12.426
removes carbonate, is the classic slip.

00:06:14.434 --> 00:06:15.073
Last one.

00:06:15.714 --> 00:06:19.233
Separate portions of another unknown give these results.

00:06:19.794 --> 00:06:22.673
Identify the anion, and write a net ionic

00:06:22.834 --> 00:06:24.553
equation for the precipitate.

00:06:24.834 --> 00:06:26.193
Pause here to answer.

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:40.360
No fizz rules out carbonate, and no barium

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:42.040
solid rules out sulfate.

00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:45.160
A cream silver solid that dissolves only in

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:47.960
concentrated ammonia is silver bromide.

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:52.360
So silver plus bromide gives solid silver bromide.

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:55.400
Calling it iodide because it looked yellowish is

00:06:55.480 --> 00:06:59.080
the trap: silver iodide wouldn't dissolve, even in

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:00.520
concentrated ammonia.

00:07:02.534 --> 00:07:03.733
And our prep room bottle?

00:07:04.134 --> 00:07:07.253
With acidified barium, it gave a white precipitate,

00:07:07.334 --> 00:07:08.853
so it's copper sulfate.

00:07:09.254 --> 00:07:12.453
Cation, then anion, and you've named the salt.

00:07:12.774 --> 00:07:15.893
Next, you'll do it for real: unknown solutions,

00:07:16.214 --> 00:07:18.613
controls and safe small-scale tests.

00:07:20.634 --> 00:07:22.073
Here are the key notes.

00:07:22.314 --> 00:07:24.233
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:24.314 --> 00:07:25.113
to copy them.
